  "admission_boundary": "Target-bound durable consumption precedes preflight/process allocation; a distinct runtime binding and input hash must match the one armed row. Competing consumers, lost consumption/arm acknowledgements, output collisions, source/input drift and revoked approvals cannot create another session. A known fully retired unarmed failure can release the target slot while its artifact remains consumed. Native uncertainty/controller loss keeps the target held.",
  "classification": "Actual Windows jobs, current-file locks, Python controller/owner/frontends and surrogate stop commands; VM facts in runtime tests are synthetic replay. Actual native read-only preflight is separate.",
  "clock_boundary": "Human review allocates no processes/jobs/lease. After explicit exact native approval, a bounded read-only worker hashes and retains the same file handles; the original owner and immutable at-most-600-second wall/monotonic lease are constructed afterward. Startup/heartbeat/stop bounds remain unchanged. Actual Python test leases were shortened to 30 seconds; the 600-second maximum also has direct conformance checks.",
  "decision": "0006; preparation approved in conversation November 24, 2026",
  "diagnostic_followup": {
    "SQLite": "One developing normal Python case held on SQLite locked; its exact failing phase was not retained. Startup BEGIN/body/COMMIT phases now retain SQLite codes without retries or changed bounds. Controlled failure traces and the retained complete regression pass. This does not prove contention-free startup or reconstruct the earlier failure.",
    "owner_packet": "Source review found the original-owner sealed packet uses owner, not process. Corrected controller-loss proof and verified it against the actual Python owner packet in the whole binder test."
  },
